Feature: Automate migration of eligible files from Purgo S3 landing folder to archive folder using Databricks PySpark

  The script must:
  - Reference the s3_file_process_log table in Unity Catalog (purgo_databricks.purgo_playground)
  - Move only files with file_status = 'SUCCESS'
  - Dynamically read s3_landing_path and s3_archive_path for each file from the log table
  - Use Databricks secrets "access_key" and "secret_key" from scope "aws_keys" for S3 access
  - Handle error scenarios such as missing files, permission issues, and duplicate files in archive

  Background:
    Given the Databricks secret scope "aws_keys" contains "access_key" and "secret_key"
    And the Unity Catalog is "purgo_databricks" and schema is "purgo_playground"
    And the table "s3_file_process_log" contains columns: file_id (string), file_name (string), file_status (string), s3_landing_path (string), s3_archive_path (string), processed_timestamp (timestamp)
    And the S3 path format is "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"

  Scenario: Move eligible files from landing to archive folder (happy path)
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 123     | patient_20240601.csv | SUCCESS     | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
    And the file "patient_20240601.csv" exists in "s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw"
    When the script is executed
    Then the file "patient_20240601.csv" is moved from "s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw" to "s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive"
    And the file no longer exists in "s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw"
    And the file exists in "s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive"

  Scenario Outline: Do not move files with non-SUCCESS status
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | <file_status> | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" is not moved to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" remains in "<s3_landing_path>"

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 124     | patient_20240602.csv | FAILED      | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
      | 125     | patient_20240603.csv | IN_PROGRESS | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when file does not exist in landing folder
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And the file "<file_name>" does not exist in "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then an error is logged with message "File <file_name> not found in <s3_landing_path>"
    And the file is not moved to "<s3_archive_path>"

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 126     | patient_20240604.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when file already exists in archive folder
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" already exists in "<s3_archive_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then an error is logged with message "File <file_name> already exists in <s3_archive_path>"
    And the file is not moved from "<s3_landing_path>"

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 127     | patient_20240605.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when S3 credentials are missing or invalid
    Given the Databricks secret scope "aws_keys" does not contain valid "access_key" and "secret_key"
    And the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    When the script is executed
    Then an error is logged with message "Invalid or missing AWS credentials in Databricks secret scope aws_keys"
    And no files are moved

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 128     | patient_20240606.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when S3 permissions are insufficient
    Given the Databricks secret scope "aws_keys" contains "access_key" and "secret_key" with insufficient S3 permissions
    And the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then an error is logged with message "Insufficient S3 permissions to move file <file_name> from <s3_landing_path> to <s3_archive_path>"
    And the file is not moved

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 129     | patient_20240607.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when source and target S3 paths are identical
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And "<s3_landing_path>" is equal to "<s3_archive_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then an error is logged with message "Source and target S3 paths are identical for file <file_name>"
    And the file is not moved

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 130     | patient_20240608.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw     |

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven migration of multiple eligible files
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains multiple files with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | SUCCESS     | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    And each file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the script is executed
    Then each file "<file_name>" is moved from "<s3_landing_path>" to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And each file no longer exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And each file exists in "<s3_archive_path>"

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 131     | patient_20240609.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
      | 132     | patient_20240610.csv | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario: Validation of S3 path format in log table
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 133     | patient_20240611.csv | SUCCESS     | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
    When the script reads the S3 paths
    Then the S3 paths must match the format "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"
    And if the format is invalid, an error is logged with message "Invalid S3 path format for file <file_name>"

  Scenario Outline: Validation rules for file_status
    Given the s3_file_process_log table contains a file with
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | <file_status> | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    When the script checks file_status
    Then file_status must be one of ["SUCCESS", "FAILED", "IN_PROGRESS"]
    And if file_status is not valid, an error is logged with message "Invalid file_status '<file_status>' for file <file_name>"

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 134     | patient_20240612.csv | ARCHIVED    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
